Do not require a registered IOPlatformExpert to mount the root filesystem.

The root filesystem now mounts:

    hfs: mounted iOS on device b(2, 0)

and the boot ends on the next line:

    panic(cpu 0 caller 0x...): iokit/bsddev/IOKitBSDInit.cpp:739
    Assertion failed: pe

That line is in IOSecureBSDRoot(), which bsd_init() calls right after
vfs_mountroot() to ask the platform whether the root device is secure:

    matching = IOService::serviceMatching("IOPlatformExpert");
    pe = (IOPlatformExpert *) IOService::waitForMatchingService(matching, 30ULL * kSecondScale);
    matching->release();
    assert(pe);

Nothing matches. IOPlatformExpert::start() does install itself as gIOPlatform

    setPlatform( this );
    gIOPlatform = this;

but it never calls registerService(), and matching only ever sees services
that have. On a real device this is invisible: the platform expert there is
AppleARMPlatform, a prelinked kext, and it registers itself. This kernel has
no kexts; its platform expert is the stock IOPlatformExpert, started from the
personality iokit-platform-personality.patch adds to KernelConfigTables.cpp.
So the wait runs its full timeout and returns NULL while the object it is
waiting for has been sitting in gIOPlatform since IOKit came up.

The timeout is 30 seconds. It arrives in about one here, because
pe_identify_machine.c hardcodes tclk = 1000000 for bcm2837-io while this SoC
counts at roughly 26 MHz, so every deadline in the kernel is reached about 26
times too early. That is a separate problem and not this patch's business --
it is noted because it explains why the panic looks instant.

So the platform expert is asked for directly, with getPlatform(), before
waiting for one to be matched. Where a platform expert does register this
changes nothing: by the time bsd_init mounts root, bsd_autoconf() has long
since run, and if somehow it has not, gIOPlatform is NULL and the wait happens
exactly as before. Where one never registers, the answer is immediate instead
of a timeout and a panic.

getPlatform() does not retain, so the result is retained to balance the
OSSafeReleaseNULL() at the end of the function.

A platform expert that is missing entirely is no longer fatal either. The
function's own logic already has a name for a platform that cannot answer the
question -- kIOReturnUnsupported, which it handles by doing nothing -- and
having nobody to ask is the same situation. Only kIOReturnNotPrivileged does
anything, and what it does is mdevremoveall(): it tears down the memory
devices, which on this machine would throw away the root filesystem. Guessing
that answer is the one thing this code must not do.
--- a/iokit/bsddev/IOKitBSDInit.cpp
+++ b/iokit/bsddev/IOKitBSDInit.cpp
@@ -732,11 +732,48 @@
 	OSDictionary     *matching;
 	const OSSymbol   *functionName = OSSymbol::withCStringNoCopy("SecureRootName");
 
-	matching = IOService::serviceMatching("IOPlatformExpert");
-	assert(matching);
-	pe = (IOPlatformExpert *) IOService::waitForMatchingService(matching, 30ULL * kSecondScale);
-	matching->release();
-	assert(pe);
+	/*
+	 * Ask the platform expert directly before waiting for one to be
+	 * matched. IOPlatformExpert::start() installs itself as gIOPlatform,
+	 * which is what getPlatform() returns, but it never calls
+	 * registerService(). On a real device that does not matter, because
+	 * the platform expert is AppleARMPlatform, a kext, and it registers
+	 * itself; the wait below is there for the case where it has not
+	 * finished matching yet.
+	 *
+	 * This kernel has no kexts. Its platform expert is the stock
+	 * IOPlatformExpert, started from the personality in
+	 * KernelConfigTables.cpp, so nothing ever matches "IOPlatformExpert",
+	 * the wait runs its full timeout and returns NULL, and the assert
+	 * below ends the boot immediately after the root filesystem mounted.
+	 * The object exists the whole time; it is simply not visible to
+	 * matching.
+	 *
+	 * Asking first also removes a 30 second stall from every boot in that
+	 * case, and changes nothing where a platform expert does register: by
+	 * this point bsd_autoconf() has long since run, and if it has not,
+	 * gIOPlatform is NULL and the wait happens exactly as before.
+	 */
+	pe = IOService::getPlatform();
+	if (pe) {
+		pe->retain();           /* balance the release below */
+	} else {
+		matching = IOService::serviceMatching("IOPlatformExpert");
+		assert(matching);
+		pe = (IOPlatformExpert *) IOService::waitForMatchingService(matching, 30ULL * kSecondScale);
+		matching->release();
+	}
+
+	if (!pe) {
+		/*
+		 * No platform expert at all. That is the kIOReturnUnsupported
+		 * case below -- nobody to answer the question -- and that case
+		 * does nothing, so neither does this.
+		 */
+		functionName->release();
+		return;
+	}
+
 	// Returns kIOReturnNotPrivileged is the root device is not secure.
 	// Returns kIOReturnUnsupported if "SecureRootName" is not implemented.
 	result = pe->callPlatformFunction(functionName, false, (void *)rootName, (void *)0, (void *)0, (void *)0);
